---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeCrontab from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeCrontab.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="crontab-generator">
  <DevEncodeCrontab client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The five fields</h2>
    <pre><code>┌───────── minute        0–59
│ ┌─────── hour          0–23
│ │ ┌───── day of month  1–31
│ │ │ ┌─── month         1–12 or JAN–DEC
│ │ │ │ ┌─ day of week   0–6 or SUN–SAT (7 also means Sunday)
│ │ │ │ │
* * * * *  command to run</code></pre>
    <p>
      In a field, <code>*</code> means every value, <code>*/n</code> means every nth,
      <code>a-b</code> is a range, and commas separate a list. So <code>0,30 9-17 * * 1-5</code> is
      &ldquo;on the hour and half hour, between 9am and 5pm, Monday to Friday&rdquo;.
    </p>

    <h2>The day-of-month / day-of-week trap</h2>
    <p>
      When both fields are restricted, cron runs the job when <strong>either</strong> matches, not
      when both match. <code>0 0 13 * 5</code> thus runs on each 13th of the month and also on each
      Friday: about 60 times a year, not once or twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is documented behaviour from Vixie cron, and it surprises almost everyone. If you need an
      AND, schedule the broader condition and test the narrower one in the command:
      <code>0 0 13 * * [ "$(date +\%u)" = 5 ] &amp;&amp; /path/to/job</code>. The description above
      the builder shows which rule applies to your expression.
    </p>

    <h2>Timezones</h2>
    <p>
      cron uses the local timezone of the machine unless you set another, and this causes most
      &ldquo;it ran an hour early&rdquo; incidents. Daylight-saving transitions add two hazards: a job
      scheduled in the hour that is skipped in spring may not run, and a job in the repeated hour in
      autumn may run twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reliable options, best first: run the server in UTC and convert in your head; set
      <code>CRON_TZ=Europe/London</code> at the top of the crontab if your cron supports it; or use a
      systemd timer with <code>Persistent=true</code>, which also catches up after downtime. The
      timezone selector above shows the next ten runs in the zone you choose, so you can see the
      difference before you install the job.
    </p>

    <h2>Special strings</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Shorthand</th><th>Equivalent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>@yearly</code> / <code>@annually</code></td><td><code>0 0 1 1 *</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>@monthly</code></td><td><code>0 0 1 * *</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>@weekly</code></td><td><code>0 0 * * 0</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>@daily</code> / <code>@midnight</code></td><td><code>0 0 * * *</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>@hourly</code></td><td><code>0 * * * *</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>@reboot</code></td><td>Once at start-up; no schedule equivalent</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why a correct expression still fails</h2>
    <p>
      Usually the schedule is not the problem. cron runs your command with an almost empty environment:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        PATH is minimal, usually <code>/usr/bin:/bin</code>. Use absolute paths for the interpreter and
        the script: <code>/usr/bin/python3 /opt/app/job.py</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        No shell profile is loaded, so nothing from <code>.bashrc</code> or <code>.profile</code>
        exists, including virtualenv activation, nvm and exported credentials.
      </li>
      <li>
        The working directory is the home directory of the user, not the location of the script. Use
        absolute paths for data files too, or <code>cd</code> first.
      </li>
      <li>
        A bare <code>%</code> is a newline in crontab syntax and must be escaped as <code>\%</code>.
        This most often breaks <code>date +%Y-%m-%d</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Output goes to email. If mail is not configured, the output is lost. Redirect it:
        <code>&gt;&gt; /var/log/job.log 2&gt;&amp;1</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Testing a job</h2>
    <p>
      Run the job as cron runs it, not as your shell does:
      <code>env -i /bin/sh -c '/full/path/to/command'</code> removes your environment and shows most
      cron-only failures immediately. Then check the schedule with the next-run table above, and only
      then install it with <code>crontab -e</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Quartz is a different syntax</h2>
    <p>
      Java schedulers (Quartz, Spring <code>@Scheduled</code>, Jenkins in some configurations) use six
      or seven fields with seconds first, require <code>?</code> in the unconstrained one of
      day-of-month or day-of-week, and number weekdays 1–7 starting at Sunday. Quartz mode above adds
      the seconds field, but the run times still use Unix rules, so use them only as a guide for
      Quartz.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
